  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday..\n\nA low pressure system moving through northern MT is expected to\nbring breezy conditions today as it departs northeast into Canada.\nWest wind gusts of 20-30 mph are expected through the day as a\nresult. As the week progresses into Tuesday, energy moving up \nunder southwest flow from a low sitting over the Pacific NW coast \nwill move up from Wyoming. This is expected to bring low \nprecipitation chances to the MT/WY border Tuesday morning, \nlifting north through the day. Isolated to scattered showers and a\nfew thunderstorms are expected. Given instability is expected to \nbe minimal, only a few weak thunderstorms are expected. Although \nthe region remains under southwest flow aloft with weak energy \nmoving through, there isn't a really strong signal for forcing. \nThere is a decent signal for a frontal passage Wednesday with some\ninstability around, but with lack of east winds in the low levels\nand uncertainty in forcing, it would just be a breezy, dry \nfrontal passage. The remainder of the week looks to be uncertain \nas the region remains under southwest flow, but ensembles seem to \nretreat energy west as ridging over the central plains expand \nwestward, diminishing the chance for precipitation.\n\nHigh temperatures are forecasted to be in the 70s and low 80s\nthrough Tuesday, warming into the 80s and low 90s through the\nweekend as high pressure builds in from the east. TS",
